-- A small 3-floor elevator — STARTER (fill in the TODOs).
--
-- The state and the `moving` logic are given. You must:
--   (1) make the door closed in any step where the car will be moving;
--   (2) make the car move one floor only when `moving` is true;
--   (3) write the safety spec "moving implies the door is closed".
--
-- This is a fill-in-the-blank version of elevator.smv. The spots marked TODO
-- are placeholders (intentionally too simple) for you to replace. As written
-- it still PARSES and runs, but TODO 1 lets the door be open while moving, so
-- the intended safety property would not hold yet.
-- Operator reminder: ! = not, & = and, | = or, -> = "implies".
MODULE main          -- single top-level component; "main" is the entry point
VAR
  floor  : 0..2;            -- floor the car is on: 0, 1, or 2
  door   : {open, closed};  -- enumerated type: exactly one of these values
  moving : boolean;         -- TRUE while the car is in motion

ASSIGN
  init(floor)  := 0;        -- start on the ground floor...
  init(door)   := open;     -- ...with the door open...
  init(moving) := FALSE;    -- ...and stopped

  -- next(moving): a case picks the first true guard. The car may only START
  -- moving while the door is closed; if closed it freely chooses move or stay.
  next(moving) := case
    door = open : FALSE;          -- door open -> cannot move
    TRUE        : {FALSE, TRUE};  -- otherwise free choice (nondeterministic)
  esac;

  -- TODO 1: if next(moving) is TRUE, force the door closed; otherwise allow
  -- {open, closed}.
  -- HINT: replace the line below with a "case" whose first guard is
  -- next(moving) -> closed, and whose TRUE branch is {open, closed}.
  -- As written, the door may be open even while moving (safety not yet met).
  next(door) := {open, closed};   -- PLACEHOLDER: door changes with no rule

  -- TODO 2: when next(moving) is TRUE, move one floor (0->1, 2->1, 1->{0,2});
  -- otherwise stay put.
  -- HINT: use a "case" guarded on next(moving) & floor = N for each floor,
  -- with a final "TRUE : floor;" so the car stays put when not moving.
  next(floor) := floor;           -- PLACEHOLDER: car never actually moves

-- TODO 3: write the safety property — moving implies the door is closed.
-- INVARSPEC is a safety claim that must hold in EVERY reachable state.
-- Uncomment the line below once TODO 1 is done (it should then pass):
-- INVARSPEC moving -> door = closed;

-- These help you sanity-check once TODO 1/2 are done. CTLSPEC AG EF p means
-- "from every reachable state, p is still reachable on some path" (A=all paths,
-- E=some path, G=always, F=eventually). With the placeholders these may behave
-- oddly (e.g. the car never leaves floor 0); they should pass once TODOs work.
CTLSPEC AG EF (floor = 2);   -- top floor stays reachable
CTLSPEC AG EF (door = open); -- an open door stays reachable
